WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:02.040
- All right, so we wanted
to answer the question

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:02.873
once and for all.

00:00:02.873 --> 00:00:06.270
What is the best smartphone
camera out right now?

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:07.770
Maybe you think it's the iPhone.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:09.780
Or maybe it's a Samsung flagship.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.370
Or maybe it's one of those Pixels.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:13.620
Now, in years past,
we've done a blind voting

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:15.060
using social media polls.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.700
Which was really fun and
yielded some surprising results.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.370
But that would still
depend on the match-ups

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:22.590
that I planted at the very beginning.

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.720
So I specifically put the iPhone

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.910
and the Pixel on opposite
sides of the bracket

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:30.030
expecting the best ones to
hopefully meet in the finals.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:30.863
But they never did.

00:00:30.863 --> 00:00:32.400
And there's always a bit of human input.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:33.990
It's actually possible I
could have accidentally

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.750
put the second best phone
up against the best phone

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:38.430
in the first round and it got eliminated.

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:41.580
So this time we're gonna solve
all that once and for all.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:44.160
Welcome to the "Blind
Smartphone Camera Test:

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:45.510
Scientific Edition."

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:46.830
So here's the idea.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:50.100
We took 16 smartphones that
came out during this year.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.320
The flagships, your
expected heavy hitters,

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.330
but then also some mid-rangers as well,

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.160
all the way down to the unexpected.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.560
And then we took the same exact photo

00:00:58.560 --> 00:00:59.760
with each one of them.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:00.593
Harder than it sounds

00:01:00.593 --> 00:01:02.100
because you have to hold perfectly still

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:03.510
for several minutes straight.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:04.860
But we actually did this

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:07.260
for three different types of photos.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.530
a standard daylight
photo, a low light photo,

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:12.300
and a portrait mode snap.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.250
And then we take all these
images, compress them,

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:17.760
strip them of their EXIF data,
and assign each one a letter.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.610
Now for maximum statistical confidence,

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.950
we would have to ask you guys to vote

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:26.550
on every single possible
match-up at least once.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:30.330
So A versus B, then A
versus C, then A versus D,

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:31.163
all the way through.

00:01:31.163 --> 00:01:35.070
And then B versus C, B
versus D. Over and over.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:37.380
Sixteen choose two for my stat heads.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:39.390
But that's clearly a bit too much.

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:40.470
I mean, we wanna be scientific,

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.590
but having you sit there
and vote over 100 times

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:46.590
for each category was a bit of a tall ask.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:48.450
So instead, we got a
little clever with it.

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:51.060
And we built a power ranking system,

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.980
actually an ELO rating algorithm,

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:54.030
which you might have heard of.

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:57.120
They use this in chess, they
use this in table tennis.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.100
And basically, in any
video game you've played

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.750
where there's a bunch
of different match-ups

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:01.583
all happening at once

00:02:01.583 --> 00:02:03.330
and you need to form a ranking of them.

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:05.400
But this time we'll be using it to sort of

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.800
form a power ranking
of smartphone cameras.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:10.200
So basically, when you hit
enough sanctioned match-ups,

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.210
you're given a rating based

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.430
on the ratings of your match-ups.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.530
So you, along with everybody else,

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:18.360
get shown comparisons at random

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.490
and we have this little
progress bar along the bottom.

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:22.530
You vote for the better photo each time,

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:23.730
and then after a little while,

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:24.900
we have enough information

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.050
to determine your individual, customized,

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.030
statistically significant winner.

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:31.110
So we built this whole site

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:32.760
with the ELO rating system in there.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.230
We loaded up all the photos

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:36.900
and then we actually tossed
this first into our Discord

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.120
to have some of you beta test this

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:40.380
before anyone else knew it existed.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:42.030
So we could experiment
with the data formatting

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:44.640
on the out end so we could
make sure it was all organized.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:45.870
So actually shout out to Discord

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:48.060
for sponsoring this
portion of today's video.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.970
So with the launch of their new
Server Subscription feature,

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:53.580
we opened up an exclusive, premium area

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:56.340
on our Discord server where
members can ask the team,

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.710
including me, you know,
general questions about tech

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:01.620
or any of the content and
get exclusive insider info

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:04.710
that you won't find anywhere
else like this test.

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:05.970
And also, if you're wondering,

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:08.820
Discord does it right with revenue split.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:12.540
It's 90-10, so 90% going to the creator.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.730
So your subscription directly supports us.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:16.710
And if you're a creator
looking for more information

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:17.700
on this particular feature

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.320
and adding it to your own Discord,

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.150
definitely check out the link below.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:23.100
So anyway, I dropped the site in there,

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:25.590
a bunch of you got in
there quick and voted,

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:28.800
and basically, we got the
data back in this text format.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:31.050
It looks kind of messy
at first, but actually,

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:32.850
it's super helpful and
we can work with this.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:34.650
A summary of how many times

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:37.320
every single match-up got voted on

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:38.670
and the votes for each side.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:41.010
And also since I had a lot of
help with building this site

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:42.540
and loading up all the pictures,

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:44.910
even though I know which
phones were involved,

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:47.670
I don't know which phones
took which pictures.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:50.340
So I could blind test myself.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:54.210
And it was every bit as
engaging as I was hoping.

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:56.550
I could kind of tell some of the phones

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:58.380
that I think took certain pictures.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.570
But for the most part, I
would just take a glance,

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:02.820
and if I was seeing those
two pictures side-by-side

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:04.500
on like Instagram or Twitter,

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:05.970
I'm just picking the better one.

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:08.370
No pixel peeping or thorough inspection.

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:11.640
It's just picture on the internet, vote.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:15.210
And in the end, my standard
picture winner was G,

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:18.000
my low light winner was K,

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.420
and my portrait mode winner was also K.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:23.250
So everything looked like it was working.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:25.740
The data processing was
working as we wanted.

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:29.130
So the only thing left
to do was send it live,

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:31.620
and here we go.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:34.050
So as soon as it went public,
you guys jumped right on it.

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:38.520
It peaked at around
35,000 votes per minute,

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:42.570
and then ended up totaling
more than 21 million votes

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:43.680
in about three days.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:45.720
There were essentially zero bad actors.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:48.480
And now we have a load of data.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:49.830
Many of you may have gone through this,

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:51.060
which means you've done the voting

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:52.560
and now you have your letter.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:55.290
Which means it's time to
reveal all of the smartphones

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:57.423
that were involved in this test.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.390
And here you go, A through P.

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:03.330
That's all 16 phones right
there in their order.

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:05.670
Which means the winner when
I blind took the test myself

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:09.510
for my standard photo was the Pixel 6A.

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:13.290
My winner for portrait mode and night mode

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:14.250
was the Pixel 7.

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:16.950
But what were the overall
winners according to statistics,

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:20.910
over the 20 plus million
votes according to science?

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:22.860
I don't know if there's any
other collection of data

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:24.090
like this anywhere on the internet.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:25.440
So this is super cool to get into.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:27.480
So let's dig into it.

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:30.120
So first of all, overall winner

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:32.520
so with the highest average ELO rating

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:35.130
across all three categories.

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:39.360
In third place is the Asus Zenfone 9.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:42.900
In second place is the Pixel 7 Pro.

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:46.320
And first place is the Pixel 6A,

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:49.560
the people's choice camera of 2022.

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:52.140
But there is a lot of
fascinating information

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:52.973
to unpack here.

00:05:52.973 --> 00:05:55.320
So first of all, doing
well in one category

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:57.300
did not necessarily guarantee doing well

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.640
across any of the other categories, right?

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:02.940
So the Pixel 7 Pro came in second, fourth,

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:05.100
and first in the three categories.

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:09.570
The Pixel 6A came in
third, second, and second.

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:11.820
But the Oppo Find X5 Pro actually came

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:15.000
in first for standard,
then third for low light,

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.920
and 15th for portrait mode.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:19.200
This basically means that, if you wanted,

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:23.340
you could argue for the
importance of the standard photo

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.680
over the low light or
the portrait mode photos.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:28.140
So let's say the standard
photo is like three times

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:30.690
as important to most people
as the other two categories,

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:32.160
which feels about right.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:35.190
With that weighted importance
on standard photos,

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:38.970
then the Oppo Find X5 Pro
becomes the overall winner

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:39.803
which is interesting.

00:06:39.803 --> 00:06:40.636
And in case you're wondering

00:06:40.636 --> 00:06:41.610
where the iPhone was in all this,

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:45.060
the iPhone 14 Pro tested in sixth place,

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:47.220
10th place, and fifth place.

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:49.020
But in natural curiosity, for me anyway,

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:52.200
was to ask what were the biggest losers.

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:54.960
Which ones got absolutely
destroyed the most

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:56.430
in this test?

00:06:56.430 --> 00:07:00.060
And your undisputed
winners of losing the most

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:05.060
is the Sony Xperia 1 IV
and the Moto Edge 30 Ultra.

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:08.310
These phones performed
terribly in these tests.

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:10.770
So the Sony, despite
its world-class sensor

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:12.570
and extremely advanced apps,

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:14.700
went in full auto, like
the rest of these phones,

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:15.930
did not produce good pictures

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:18.120
and ended up with the
lowest average ELO rating

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:19.890
across all three categories.

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:21.990
And then the Moto Edge 30 Ultra actually

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:24.000
had the most overall losses.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.800
It took a bizarre sequence of photos,

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:28.410
including an extremely
dark standard light photo

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:31.200
and an incredibly over
sharpened low light photo,

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:34.800
that it's the only one to
have 2 million total votes

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:35.633
against it.

00:07:35.633 --> 00:07:37.710
Now here's another interesting thought.

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:39.510
Votes per dollar.

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:42.330
If we wanted to sort by
bang for the buck basically

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:46.530
which ones, if we account for
the launch price, the MSRP,

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:50.070
which one was performing the
best relative to its price?

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:53.520
And the two winners for
votes per dollar by far

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:54.870
are the Pixel 6A.

00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:58.050
No surprise. Over 4,200 votes per dollar.

00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:01.680
And the Realme 10 Pro+
also sneaks in there,

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:04.740
the only other phone up
over 4,000 votes per dollar.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:07.380
Sneaky good camera phone for $379.

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:11.700
And then the obvious losers,
again, are the $900 Moto Edge

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:15.270
and the $1,600 Sony Xperia 1 IV.

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:16.350
Also I just had to.

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:17.580
I had to look through the text files

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:19.080
and find the biggest dunk.

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:21.960
Just the biggest overall
match-up discrepancy

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:24.120
between two phones in any category.

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:25.020
And I found it.

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:27.840
That would be portrait mode Pixel 7 Pro

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:29.850
versus Sony Xperia 1 IV

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:33.990
with the Pixel winning
98% of the votes here.

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:37.800
So to the 1,523 of you that
voted for the Sony here,

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:39.810
not sure what you saw that
the rest of us didn't.

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:42.060
But overall, just more
than seeing which ones

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:46.440
got the most votes is
seeing why certain phones

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:48.840
and certain pictures got the most votes.

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:50.070
And the best way to do that

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:52.680
was actually just
putting all of the photos

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:56.850
in order side-by-side in order
of how many votes they got.

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:58.230
And that's how we started to see

00:08:58.230 --> 00:08:59.640
some really interesting patterns.

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:01.980
So like for example, last
time in the head-to-head,

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:05.760
we kind of established that
in a case of A versus B,

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:07.920
if one vote is a little
brighter than the other,

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:09.540
it'll probably win.

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:10.560
But how far can that go?

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:12.960
Is there a such thing as
too bright in this case?

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:14.850
And it turned out, yes.

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:17.940
This is all the standard
mode photos in order.

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:20.130
And yes, the three darkest photos

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:21.660
are the three biggest losers.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:24.990
Hands down, they crushed
any shadow detail in my hair

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:27.030
and the extremely comfortable
sweater I'm wearing

00:09:27.030 --> 00:09:28.980
on shop.mkbhd.com.

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:31.260
But right above that

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:33.990
is three of the absolute brightest photos.

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:36.690
Like these were just
overexposed straight up.

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:38.700
Matter of fact, it looks like
we kind of got this order

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:43.530
of great photos, then too
bright, then too dark.

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:45.330
So it shows me that good exposure wins.

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:46.800
But if you're gonna miss,

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:48.900
then yeah, people would
rather have it be too bright

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:49.733
than too dark.

00:09:49.733 --> 00:09:51.480
With night mode altogether and in order,

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:53.880
you can kind of see a
bit of the same thing.

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:56.910
So the most neutral, correct
exposures generally won

00:09:56.910 --> 00:09:57.990
if they were sharp.

00:09:57.990 --> 00:10:00.240
This time there's about
four good exposures.

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:05.240
Vivo X80 Pro, Pixel 6A, Oppo
Find X5 Pro, and Pixel 7 Pro.

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:07.950
Then there's the two brights,

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:11.070
which people mostly
preferred over the two darks.

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:13.380
Also, there were some
seriously wild things happening

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:15.210
with some of these photos in low light.

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:17.670
The Vivo, I mean, it absolutely nailed it.

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:20.160
It's clearly the low light king.

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:23.550
The S22 ultra interestingly
came in fifth place

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:27.630
even when completely missing
focus on my stationary face

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:29.970
and hitting the background
for some reason.

00:10:29.970 --> 00:10:33.420
The iPhone 14 Pro did an
enormous amount of HDR

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:36.300
that had me looking kind
of green like a zombie,

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:38.490
but it still came in 11th place here.

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:40.020
The Moto Edge Plus, as I mentioned,

00:10:40.020 --> 00:10:42.720
kicked out maybe the
most over sharpened photo

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:44.070
the world has ever seen.

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:47.850
And then in dead last is the iPhone SE,

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:52.080
the only phone in this test
that does not have night mode.

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:54.690
And the sad thing is
like it does have OIS,

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:56.580
it does have a decent sensor,

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:58.620
and it does have a new processor.

00:10:58.620 --> 00:10:59.670
But Apple just feels the need

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:01.620
to protect their more expensive phones

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:03.120
with more premium features.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:05.580
So they didn't give the SE night mode,

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:07.590
and it suffered big time here.

00:11:07.590 --> 00:11:11.010
Then in portrait mode, we have
the most variety of them all

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:12.690
because not only are we judging things

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:14.130
that we normally would about a photo

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:16.770
like sharpness, color exposure, et cetera,

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:19.890
but we are also now adding cutout quality

00:11:19.890 --> 00:11:22.950
and artificially blurred
background quality.

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:26.850
And every phone uses a
slightly different focal length

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:28.500
for their portrait mode by default.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:31.680
Some of them do a 2x portrait
mode, some of them 2.5,

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:35.010
some of them 3x, some of them even do 1x.

00:11:35.010 --> 00:11:37.080
But combining all of these factors,

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:40.920
the Pixel 7 Pro, absolutely
dominated portrait mode.

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:43.680
This was the runaway winner
here in this category.

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:45.960
Great cutout, great exposure,

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:48.450
great colors and a nice soft background.

00:11:48.450 --> 00:11:49.740
It nailed it, but guess what?

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:52.380
The Pixel 6A was in second

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:55.470
and the Realme 10 Pro+
snuck in there in third.

00:11:55.470 --> 00:11:58.800
Right above what I
consider the best cutout

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:00.390
of the entire competition,

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:03.690
which came from this Galaxy S22 Ultra.

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:06.930
I think it's pretty close to
perfect at edge detection here.

00:12:06.930 --> 00:12:09.270
Pretty similar to the Zenfone 9's cutout,

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:11.040
but a better overall photo.

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:13.470
So it got fourth place instead of seventh.

00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:15.390
I was also curious if there
would be a correlation

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:17.460
between how many votes a phone got

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:19.080
and whether or not they used more

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:20.880
of a telephoto focal length.

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:22.830
Just because maybe it
would feel more natural

00:12:22.830 --> 00:12:25.620
having a blurred
background if you're at 3x.

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:27.090
But when we lined it all up,

00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:29.850
there was almost no trend line
at all and no correlation.

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:31.080
So that was interesting.

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:33.780
Really, the only other
definite thing I noticed

00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:35.370
about portrait mode photos

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:39.660
is that the three worst photos
that got the fewest votes

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:43.770
by far were also by far
the brightest photos.

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:46.890
So see, it turns out you
can overdo the brightness.

00:12:46.890 --> 00:12:51.030
And that, I think that is basically it.

00:12:51.030 --> 00:12:53.940
So summary time, just from
all the data that we have.

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:54.960
Things that we learned.

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:57.330
Number one is that being really good

00:12:57.330 --> 00:13:00.060
in one of these categories
did not necessarily guarantee

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:01.440
being good at the others.

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:03.690
That's clearly reflected in the data.

00:13:03.690 --> 00:13:08.690
Two, is that Pixel 6A
and the Realme 10 Pro+

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:10.800
turned out to be the
best bang for the buck

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:13.320
as far as a smartphone
camera from the phones

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:14.910
that came out in 2022.

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:17.370
And the Pixel 6A being the overall winner

00:13:17.370 --> 00:13:21.270
actually matches with it
winning our bracket style

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:24.600
social media polling
blind test from last year,

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:26.220
which the Pixel 5A won.

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:27.450
And last but not least,

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:30.240
yeah, brighter photos do typically win,

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:32.820
but you can in fact be too bright.

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:36.210
So I think this mission was
a success. We learned a lot.

00:13:36.210 --> 00:13:38.100
I'll say congrats to the Pixel 6A

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:40.770
on the People's Choice Award, so to speak.

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:41.603
And you know what?

00:13:41.603 --> 00:13:45.330
We're gonna leave up the
voting website till, let's say,

00:13:45.330 --> 00:13:46.890
through the end of this year.

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:48.570
Till the end of January how about.

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:51.000
So January 31st is the
last day you'll be able

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:55.020
to go to vote.mkbhd.com
and blind test yourself.

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:56.730
And I think we'll replace the letters now

00:13:56.730 --> 00:13:57.870
with the actual names of the phones

00:13:57.870 --> 00:13:59.370
now that it's been revealed.

00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:00.870
But we don't wanna have to pay

00:14:00.870 --> 00:14:03.120
for all this terabytes of
data processing forever.

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:04.080
So that's your deadline.

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:05.250
So thanks to y'all on the Discord

00:14:05.250 --> 00:14:07.170
for helping us beta test this thing.

00:14:07.170 --> 00:14:09.630
Thanks to Zach for
helping us build this site

00:14:09.630 --> 00:14:12.660
and implement the ELO
style power ranking system.

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:15.780
And thanks to you for watching.

00:14:15.780 --> 00:14:18.340
Catch you guys to the next one. Peace.

00:14:18.340 --> 00:14:20.757
(soft music)

